00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:29,000
cemetery, which sounds

277
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:31,000
hugely ambitious And it was,

278
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:33,000
even though these are the two smallest

279
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:35,000
battlefields

280
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:37,000
and federal cemeteries in the United States

281
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:39,000
and Balls Bluff.

282
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:41,000
But it was quite an experience doing this

283
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:43,000
and as part of it we were

284
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:45,000
going to be in the flag

285
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:47,000
ceremony in our Civil War uniforms

286
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:49,000
at

287
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:51,000
the National Jamboree And

288
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:53,000
we also marched in the Centennial

289
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:55,000
Parade of Boy Scouting

290
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:57,000
down Pennsylvania Avenue in Washington DC

291
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:59,000
and I think there was

292
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:01,000
20,000 scouts in that parade.

293
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:03,000
It was quite a parade. It was the first one of this kind

294
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:05,000
since

295
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:07,000
the first jamboree before FDR.

296
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:09,000
I was in the contingent at the very

297
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:11,000
end which was Eagle Scouts,

298
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:13,000
and each of us

299
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:15,000
was asked to

300
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:17,000
carry or wear something that represented

301
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:19,000
our career. So I had the notebook,

302
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,000
I had the satchel over my shoulder

303
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:23,000
with the press card. But I looked

304
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:25,000
around me and there were

305
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:27,000
guys dressed as department stores,

306
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
Santa Claus. There was a guy

307
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,000
from Bermuda who was a diplomat.

308
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:33,000
There was

309
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,000
a military officer and he was wearing the

310
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:37,000
full Hurt Locker bomb demolition

311
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:39,000
outfit- which was pretty tough

312
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,000
because it was 100 degrees that day-

313
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,000
Just a panoply

314
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:45,000
of distinguished

315
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:47,000
middle-aged and older

316
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:49,000
men showing the

317
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:51,000
wares of these

318
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:53,000
remarkable careers that had followed

319
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:55,000
becoming Eagle Scouts.

320
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:57,000
And we started down that parade-

321
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:59,000
and I was afraid a few of us weren't going to make it

322
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:01,000
in that heat. They were 80-year-olds, with us

323
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:03,000
marching at the tail end of

324
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:05,000
this massive parade of Boy Scouts.

325
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:07,000
We turned the corner

326
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:09,000
and started heading down towards the

327
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
Washington Monument And as we

328
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,000
did, the loudspeaker shouted out:

329
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:15,000
ladies and gentlemen,

330
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:17,000
this is Eagle Scouts.

331
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:19,000
And the roar

332
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:21,000
that came up from the crowd- there was probably

333
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,000
50 or 60,000 people lining that

334
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:25,000
mile of

335
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:27,000
avenue there, The roar that came up

336
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:29,000
for these Eagles

337
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:31,000
and looking at these old guys, you know, with bad

338
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:33,000
hips and glimping and all that.

339
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,000
And we all stood a whole lot

340
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
straighter and

341
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
marched in formation

342
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:41,000
And I suddenly was struck

343
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:43,000
by the relationship between

344
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:45,000
Eagle Scouts and American culture

345
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:47,000
And that story

346
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:49,000
had never really been explored

347
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:51,000
And I just

348
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,000
had to write the book at that point.

349
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:55,000
It was an insane idea

350
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:57,000
because I was buried in

351
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:59,000
a new start-up technology company

352
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:01,000
and writing other books

353
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:03,000
and teaching college.

354
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:05,000
But I just had to write the book because

355
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:07,000
I realized nobody else was going to do it.

356
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:09,000
There were no real plans

357
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:11,000
for the 100th anniversary of Eagle Scouting,

358
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:13,000
So I decided to do the celebration myself

359
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:15,000
And you know the result

360
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:17,000
shows the passion that you had for it.

361
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:19,000
Yeah, it's kind of a book that wrote itself.

362
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:21,000
I tore through this one.

363
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:23,000
Some books are very painful to write.

364
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:25,000
Spending

365
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:27,000
three years writing about Steve Jobs once

366
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:29,000
was not the happiest time of my life,

367
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:31,000
But writing this book

368
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:33,000
my fingers worked

369
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:35,000
by themselves. If we asked everybody

370
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:37,000
who the first Eagle Scout was,

371
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:39,000
they'd probably come up with Arthur Eldred.

372
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:41,000
But there's a lot more to

373
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:43,000
that story

374
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:45,000
And there's a lot more nuance and depth to it.

375
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:47,000
First, tell me about the time

376
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:49,000
that Arthur was a scout

377
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:51,000
and what was going on

378
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:53,000
at that point

379
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:55,000
in the history of the BSA.

380
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:57,000
Yeah, let me preface that by saying

381
00:13:57,000 --> 00:13:59,000
we all know it's Arthur Eldred,

382
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:01,000
But 15,, 20 years ago

383
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:03,000
I think, if you

384
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:05,000
asked people who the first Eagle Scout was,

385
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:07,000
nobody would know. I don't think the BSA

386
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:09,000
was quite certain.

387
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:11,000
That was a fact from basically the

388
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:13,000
20s until the 90s

389
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:15,000
that people didn't really know

390
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:17,000
about Arthur Eldred until I think it was

391
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,000
Boy's Life. our scouting magazine

392
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:21,000
went out to find who it was

393
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:23,000
And even then they got the wrong photo.

394
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:25,000
For a number of years

395
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:27,000
there was this square-jawed kid

396
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:29,000
out of a poster

397
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,000
who everyone thought was Arthur Eldred,

398
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:33,000
And finally

399
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:35,000
they found real photos of the guy,

400
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:37,000
just like every other Eagle Scout

401
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:39,000
you've ever known in your life,

402
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:41,000
Kind of skinny, smart-looking

403
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
and a bit amused

404
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:45,000
having to pose for the photograph.

405
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:47,000
You can really empathize.

406
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:49,000
He's us. Arthur Eldred is us,

407
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:51,000
except he's kind of more

408
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:53,000
than the rest of us given his career.

409
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,000
He got his Eagle in 1912

410
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:57,000
and you have to understand

411
00:14:57,000 --> 00:14:59,000
that scouting didn't begin

412
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:01,000
as a monolithic organization.

413
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:03,000
It flows out of multiple organizations

414
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:05,000
like Daniel Boone Society,

415
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:07,000
various other groups.

416
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:09,000
It starts out

417
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:11,000
as basically a fad

418
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:13,000
among teenage boys.

419
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:15,000
They hear about Boy Scouts in England

420
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:17,000
and they start their own groups

421
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:19,000
and they turn into troops

422
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:21,000
and they turn into

423
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:23,000
these sort of ad hoc organizations.

424
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:25,000
There were about four or five

425
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:27,000
different Boy Scouts organizations

426
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:29,000
in the first decade

427
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,000
of the 20th century.

428
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
They were all over the place.

429
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:35,000
and if you ever

430
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:37,000
want to read the whole history of it

431
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,000
or read the Scouting Party,

432
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:41,000
which is the story of these early days

433
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:43,000
and the clashes of egos

434
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:45,000
of the different founders and all of that,

435
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:47,000
Arthur Eldred was a boy living on Long Island.

436
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:49,000
His father had died.

437
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:51,000
His mother was concerned,

438
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:53,000
like mothers today,

439
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:55,000
that without a dad he would get in trouble.

440
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,000
He had an older brother

441
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:59,000
who was quite a bit older,

442
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:01,000
so he stepped in

443
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:03,000
as his surrogate dad

444
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:05,000
and helped raise him.

445
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:07,000
This older brother

446
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:09,000
looked around and saw these emerging

447
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:11,000
Scouting groups

448
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:13,000
and thought

449
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:15,000
why don't I build one of these groups

450
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:17,000
with Arthur in it,

451
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:19,000
and that will give structure to his life?

452
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:21,000
He went out and actually set out

453
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:23,000
to join another Scouting organization,

454
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:25,000
One that's long gone now,

455
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:27,000
but it was much more

456
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:29,000
a touristic organization.

457
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:31,000
They carried guns around, they marched

458
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:33,000
and they were sort of like preparing boys

459
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:35,000
to join the Army

460
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:37,000
because it's not a bad thing to do

461
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:39,000
in 1910, given the clouds

462
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:41,000
forming over Europe.

463
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:43,000
but he had trouble getting

464
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:45,000
permission to start

465
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:47,000
the organization or something

466
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:49,000
and instead he said: okay, well,

467
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:51,000
there's this other outfit

468
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:53,000
headquartered down in Manhattan,

469
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:55,000
why don't we join up with them?

470
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:57,000
and that's America.

471
00:16:57,000 --> 00:16:59,000
So they started this troop out on Long Island

472
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:01,000
and the boys did the usual stuff.

473
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:03,000
they didn't have a lot of money

474
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:05,000
they sort of

475
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:07,000
pulled their dough for

476
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:09,000
to buy uniforms. some of them didn't have uniforms.

477
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:11,000
it was very, very casual.

478
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:13,000
it was sort of closer to Baden Powell's

479
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:15,000
dream of what Scouting was

480
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:17,000
than what we think of today

481
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:19,000
of 60 member scout troops

482
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:21,000
meeting in a church gymnasium

483
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:23,000
with a trailer full of stuff

484
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:25,000
and all of that, and this was boys meeting

485
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,000
in the evening and like in a barn

486
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:29,000
or something.

487
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:31,000
and Arthur. at the time

488
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:33,000
there was very few

489
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:35,000
documentation on how

490
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:37,000
to be a Boy Scout.

491
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:39,000
there weren't really any ranks at the time.

492
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:41,000
it was all in development

493
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:43,000
and so

494
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:45,000
when the

495
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:47,000
first Boy Scout handbook appeared,

496
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:49,000
boys across America-

497
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:51,000
there was probably

498
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:53,000
1000 boys across the United States

499
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:55,000
that were involved in the organization at that time.

500
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:57,000
they gobbled up that book

501
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:59,000
because it not only showed them all the Scouting skills.

502
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:01,000
I mean, they had been dealing,

503
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:03,000
working with Baden Powell's old book

504
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:05,000
and various other books

505
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:07,000
about the outdoors. now they had an official book

506
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:09,000
and they went at it,

507
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:11,000
they consumed it

508
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:13,000
and began earning the various ranks.

509
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:15,000
and the ranks were kind of what they are today,

510
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:17,000
up until first class.

511
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:19,000
so you had 10% class

512
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:21,000
up until first class

513
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:23,000
and then it all kind of went crazy

514
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:25,000
because there were merit badges,

515
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:27,000
but there was only about 15 merit badges

516
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:29,000
and they were divided in two different categories.

517
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:31,000
there was sort of like an outdoor category

518
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:33,000
and then there was the home

519
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:35,000
and citizenship category

520
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:37,000
and you either earned

521
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:39,000
the star or the life.

522
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:41,000
they weren't sequential,

523
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:43,000
they were kind of in opposition.

524
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:45,000
and then there was this thing called the Eagle Scout Award

525
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:47,000
and this was the first time these boys

526
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:49,000
have ever seen it,

527
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:51,000
and Boy Scouts didn't give a lot of information,

528
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:53,000
they just sort of said

529
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:55,000
you had to have a certain number of merit badges.

530
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:57,000
and they had a drawing

531
00:18:57,000 --> 00:18:59,000
of the Eagle Medal

532
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:01,000
and it was

533
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:03,000
very different from what we think of as the Eagle Medal.

534
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:05,000
it was a straight ribbon

535
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:07,000
with one color, monochromatic.

536
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:09,000
they didn't know what color it was.

537
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:11,000
it was a black and white book

538
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:13,000
and an eagle in flight flying sideways,

539
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:15,000
and that's all they knew.

540
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:17,000
they didn't get to the Eagle yet

541
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:19,000
available, but Boy Scouts

542
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:21,000
promised they'd have more.

543
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:23,000
so that's kind of where things stood.

544
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:25,000
so all across America, boys were earning these ranks.

545
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:27,000
well, Arthur Elder was one of these singular characters

546
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:29,000
and

547
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:31,000
he sort of

548
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:33,000
is the embodiment

549
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:35,000
of what we think of as that overachieving Eagle Scout.

550
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:37,000
Eagle Scouts are inevitably.

551
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:39,000
I've mentored about 50 of them.

552
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:41,000
I know you have mentored probably at least that many.

553
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:43,000
these kids

554
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:45,000
are focused and they,

555
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:47,000
they go for it.

556
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:49,000
and if Scouting had said you have to have 150 merit badges,

557
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:51,000
we still have Eagle Scouts

558
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:53,000
because these are the kinds of young men

559
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:55,000
who are goal oriented

560
00:19:55,000 --> 00:19:57,000
and they'll do it. whatever it takes,

561
00:19:57,000 --> 00:19:59,000
